><button type="button" tabindex="0" data-base-ui-click-trigger id="base-ui-_R_7tba_" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" data-slot="sheet-trigger" aria-label="Open menu" class="flex h-10 cursor-pointer touch-manipulation items-center gap-2 border bg-card px-3 font-mono text-sm text-foreground sm:hidden">menu<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-menu size-4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 5h16"/><path d="M4 12h16"/><path d="M4 19h16"/></svg></button></div><main><div data-rail="true" class="min-[1152px]:grid min-[1152px]:grid-cols-[48rem_minmax(0,1fr)] min-[1152px]:gap-x-12"><div class="min-w-0"><header class="max-w-3xl pt-8 pb-10 sm:pt-24"><p class="font-mono text-sm text-muted-foreground tabular-nums"><b class="font-medium text-primary">№ <!-- -->37</b> · <!-- -->2026-11-05<!-- --> ·<!-- --> <!-- -->12<!-- --> min ·<!-- --> <a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../server">server</a></p><h1 class="mt-3 max-w-[24ch] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3.5vw+0.8rem,3rem)] leading-[1.15] font-normal tracking-tight">*sshole: how * became a password in OpenSSH on musl</h1><p class="mt-6 max-w-[52ch] text-xl font-light text-muted-foreground">sshd on Alpine and other musl systems took any 257 to 1,024 byte password for accounts with * in /etc/shadow. Who is exposed, why, and what to do.</p><div class="mt-10 max-w-md" aria-label="Entries over the past year, with this one marked"><svg class="block h-auto w-full overflow-visible" viewBox="0 0 480 40" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0.0,34.0 L9.2,34.0 L18.5,34.0 L27.7,34.0 L36.9,34.0 L46.2,34.0 L55.4,34.0 L64.6,34.0 L73.8,34.0 L83.1,34.0 L92.3,34.0 L101.5,34.0 L110.8,34.0 L120.0,34.0 L129.2,34.0 L138.5,34.0 L147.7,34.0 L156.9,34.0 L166.2,34.0 L175.4,34.0 L184.6,34.0 L193.8,34.0 L203.1,34.0 L212.3,34.0 L221.5,34.0 L230.8,34.0 L240.0,34.0 L249.2,34.0 L258.5,34.0 L267.7,34.0 L276.9,34.0 L286.2,34.0 L295.4,34.0 L304.6,34.0 L313.8,34.0 L323.1,34.0 L332.3,34.0 L341.5,34.0 L350.8,34.0 L360.0,34.0 L369.2,6.0 L378.5,34.0 L387.7,34.0 L396.9,27.8 L406.2,34.0 L415.4,34.0 L424.6,34.0 L433.8,30.9 L443.1,30.9 L452.3,24.7 L461.5,34.0 L470.8,34.0 L480.0,34.0" class="fill-none stroke-border" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="450.3296703296703" cy="26.000000000000007" r="4" class="fill-primary"/></svg><p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs tracking-wider text-muted-foreground uppercase">you are here<!-- --> · entry <!-- -->37<!-- --> <!-- -->of <!-- -->37<!-- --> · <!-- -->16<!-- --> in <!-- -->the past year</p></div><ul class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground"><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../security">#<!-- -->security</a></li><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../linux">#<!-- -->linux</a></li><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../openssh">#<!-- -->openssh</a></li></ul></header><article class="prose-ui w-full max-w-3xl pb-10"><p>For ten years, <code>sshd</code> on some musl Linux systems accepted a password for accounts that have no password. Send any 257 to 1,024 bytes and the account opens. <a href="https://alpinelinux.org/posts/2026-11-05-openssh-password-authentication-bypass/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Alpine&#x27;s advisory</a> puts it plainly: &quot;It takes one connection, works every time, and needs no retries.&quot;</p>
<p><strong>If you run <code>sshd</code> on Alpine, update <code>openssh</code> and then restart <code>sshd</code>.</strong> The upgrade alone leaves the old daemon running.</p>
<p>Özgün Kültekin of <a href="https://wakefield.co" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wakefield</a> found it and named it <a href="https://sshhole.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">*sshole</a>. OpenSSH shipped a workaround in 10.6p2 on 5 November 2026. No CVE had been assigned when Alpine published, and I haven&#x27;t found one since.</p>
<p>Nobody wrote this bug on purpose. Different people made five sensible decisions between 1980 and 2022, and one character connects them.</p>
<h2 id="am-i-affected">Am I affected?</h2>
<p>Answer these five questions. You are exposed only if all five are true.</p>
<div data-article-component="StarExposure" data-state="fallback"><section aria-label="Am I affected?" class="not-prose pb-4 article-frame my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] text-syntax-text"><div class="not-prose flex min-h-11 items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b px-3 font-mono text-xs border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><span class="min-w-0 truncate text-syntax-text">am i affected?</span><span class="flex shrink-0 items-center text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">5<!-- --> conditions</span></div><p class="mt-3 px-3 text-sm">You are exposed only if all five of these are true:</p><ol class="mt-3 grid gap-3 px-3"><li class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-2 text-sm leading-relaxed"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">01</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">Does sshd run on musl libc?</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">Alpine, and OpenWrt, Buildroot or Yocto builds that use OpenSSH. <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">ldd /usr/sbin/sshd</code> shows it.</span></span></li><li class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-2 text-sm leading-relaxed"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">02</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">Does sshd check passwords without PAM?</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">Alpine&#x27;s openssh-server is built without PAM. openssh-server-pam with UsePAM no counts too.</span></span></li><li class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-2 text-sm leading-relaxed"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">03</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">Are password logins on?</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">PasswordAuthentication yes is the default. cloud-init images turn it off.</span></span></li><li class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-2 text-sm leading-relaxed"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">04</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">Does an account have exactly * in /etc/shadow?</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">Key-only users, root on Alpine 3.16 and later, the alpine user on tiny-cloud images. Not !, which stays locked.</span></span></li><li class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-2 text-sm leading-relaxed"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">05</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">Does another account have a real $5$ or $6$ hash?</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">One user with a password is enough, such as an admin who set a console password.</span></span></li></ol></section></div>
<p>These commands check them on a running box:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">ldd</span><span class="hl-s"> /usr/sbin/sshd</span><span class="hl-k"> |</span><span class="hl-f"> grep</span><span class="hl-s"> -i</span><span class="hl-s"> musl</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">sshd</span><span class="hl-s"> -T</span><span class="hl-k"> |</span><span class="hl-f"> grep</span><span class="hl-s"> -Ei</span><span class="hl-se"> '^(usepam|passwordauthentication|permitrootlogin)'</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">awk</span><span class="hl-s"> -F:</span><span class="hl-se"> '$2 == "*" {print $1}'</span><span class="hl-s"> /etc/shadow</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">awk</span><span class="hl-s"> -F:</span><span class="hl-se"> '$2 ~ /^\$[56]\$/ {print $1}'</span><span class="hl-s"> /etc/shadow</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p>A build without PAM prints no <code>usepam</code> line at all.</p>
<p>The fifth condition surprises people. One user with a password makes every <code>*</code> account on the machine reachable. Some early coverage also overstates the reach, so three details:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Containers:</strong> running an Alpine image doesn&#x27;t expose you by itself. The advisory leaves out containers that don&#x27;t run <code>sshd</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Root:</strong> Alpine&#x27;s root has had <code>*</code> since about 3.16, but the default <code>PermitRootLogin prohibit-password</code> protects it. Root is reachable directly only with <code>PermitRootLogin yes</code>. An attacker who gets into another account can still become root, for example with <code>doas</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Cloud images:</strong> the <code>alpine</code> user in tiny-cloud images has a <code>*</code> field and passwordless <code>doas</code>. The advisory says these images are safe on first boot and become exploitable as soon as any <code>$6$</code> hash exists, for example when an admin sets a console password.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not affected: images set up by cloud-init (it writes <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code>), glibc systems, non-Linux systems, <code>UsePAM yes</code>, and Dropbear, which is the default SSH server on OpenWrt. Accounts locked with <code>!</code> can&#x27;t log in with a password or a key, so they&#x27;re out too.</p>
<h2 id="five-decisions-one-character">Five decisions, one character</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://sshhole.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">*sshole write-up</a> tells this story as a comic, and it&#x27;s worth reading. Here&#x27;s the short version, with the details checked in the source.</p>
<div data-article-component="Stepper" data-state="fallback"><section aria-label="five decisions, 1980 to 2022" class="article-frame my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] text-syntax-text pb-2"><div class="not-prose flex min-h-11 items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b px-3 font-mono text-xs border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><span class="min-w-0 truncate text-syntax-text">five decisions, 1980 to 2022</span><span class="flex shrink-0 items-center text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">6<!-- --> steps</span></div><div class="px-3"><ol class="steps mt-4! [&amp;_.step-indicator]:rounded-none!" data-title-size="base"><li class="step"><div class="step-indicator" aria-hidden="true"><span class="step-number"></span></div><div class="step-content"><p class="step-title">1980: * means nobody logs in here</p><div class="step-body"><p>Early Unix kept password hashes in <code>/etc/passwd</code>. In 3BSD the <code>daemon</code> account has a <code>*</code> in that field. A <code>*</code> is not a valid hash, so no password can produce it. The write-up credits Bill Joy, and Unix still uses the convention.</p></div></div></li><li class="step"><div class="step-indicator" aria-hidden="true"><span class="step-number"></span></div><div class="step-content"><p class="step-title">1997: crypt() returns NULL on failure</p><div class="step-body"><p>The Single Unix Specification says so. Plenty of programs never checked, compared the result anyway and crashed.</p></div></div></li><li class="step"><div class="step-indicator" aria-hidden="true"><span class="step-number"></span></div><div class="step-content"><p class="step-title">9 August 2012: musl stops returning NULL</p><div class="step-body"><p>Rich Felker changed musl so that a failed <code>crypt()</code> returns a string that can&#x27;t match. <a href="https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=b3c4cc121f70faea45389fe7ddc1127ed5cbd8bb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The commit</a> calls this &quot;reportedly a better behavior than failing on error&quot;. In <code>crypt_des.c</code> the failure path is one line:</p><div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">return</span><span class="hl-fg"> (setting[</span><span class="hl-c">0</span><span class="hl-fg">]</span><span class="hl-k">==</span><span class="hl-se">'*'</span><span class="hl-fg">) </span><span class="hl-k">?</span><span class="hl-se"> "x"</span><span class="hl-k"> :</span><span class="hl-se"> "*"</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>If the setting is <code>*</code>, it returns <code>x</code>, so the result never equals the setting it was given. In 2012 that was enough, because callers passed the stored hash as the setting.</p></div></div></li><li class="step"><div class="step-indicator" aria-hidden="true"><span class="step-number"></span></div><div class="step-content"><p class="step-title">29 August 2012: a 256-byte cap</p><div class="step-body"><p><a href="https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=88bf5a8a8d7d796f63cca8589f4de67aa8345f1a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Twenty days later</a> musl added SHA-256 and SHA-512 crypt. These get slower as the password gets longer, so musl refuses long keys. The source says why: &quot;key limit is not part of the original design, added for DoS protection&quot;. Above 256 bytes these functions fail, and failure now means <code>*</code>.</p></div></div></li><li class="step"><div class="step-indicator" aria-hidden="true"><span class="step-number"></span></div><div class="step-content"><p class="step-title">15 July 2016: OpenSSH borrows a salt</p><div class="step-body"><p><a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/commit/9286875a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CVE-2016-6210</a> was a timing leak: send long passwords, time the replies, and you learn which usernames exist. Darren Tucker&#x27;s fix hashes with a salt taken from a real account, so every failure takes as long.</p><p>The fix was meant for invalid users. One condition also sends real accounts down the new path when their stored field is too short to be a salt:</p><div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">if</span><span class="hl-fg"> (authctxt</span><span class="hl-k">-></span><span class="hl-fg">valid </span><span class="hl-k">&#x26;&#x26;</span><span class="hl-fg"> pw_password[</span><span class="hl-c">0</span><span class="hl-fg">] </span><span class="hl-k">&#x26;&#x26;</span><span class="hl-fg"> pw_password[</span><span class="hl-c">1</span><span class="hl-fg">])</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-fg">        salt </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-fg"> pw_password;</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-fg">encrypted_password </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-f"> xcrypt</span><span class="hl-fg">(password</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-fg"> salt);</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Before this, a one-character field got the DES salt <code>xx</code>, and DES on musl has no key limit. After it, a <code>*</code> account gets a borrowed <code>$6$</code> salt. The setting and the stored field are now different strings, so musl&#x27;s <code>x</code> guard never fires. The bug starts here.</p></div></div></li><li class="step"><div class="step-indicator" aria-hidden="true"><span class="step-number"></span></div><div class="step-content"><p class="step-title">2019 to 2022: Alpine moves root to *</p><div class="step-body"><p>CVE-2019-5021 was an empty root password in Alpine&#x27;s Docker images. Alpine locked root with <code>!</code>. Without PAM, <code>!</code> also blocks SSH keys, so Alpine 3.16 changed root to <code>*</code> in 2022. That didn&#x27;t cause the bug, but it made <code>*</code> accounts normal on the distribution most likely to meet the other conditions.</p></div></div></li></ol></div></section></div>
<p>As far as anyone knows, nobody noticed for ten years. The write-up gives the reasons: OpenSSH tests on OpenBSD and glibc, and musl doesn&#x27;t test <code>sshd</code>. Most distributions use PAM, which never reaches this code. And a box with no real password hash is safe, because the salt falls back to <code>xx</code>.</p>
<p>Two threads came close. In 2013 Thorsten Glaser of Debian <a href="https://sourceware.org/legacy-ml/libc-alpha/2013-07/msg00046.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">asked glibc</a> to return <code>*</code> on failure the way musl does, in <a href="https://bugs.debian.org/714219" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Debian bug 714219</a>. Debian later moved to libxcrypt, which returns <code>*0</code>, one character away. In December 2024, a <a href="https://www.openwall.com/lists/musl/2024/12/29/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">musl list thread</a> dealt with <code>passwd</code> crashing on Alpine when PAM tried to overwrite musl&#x27;s <code>*</code> after a password longer than 256 characters. Felker <a href="https://www.openwall.com/lists/musl/2024/12/31/2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">defended the limit</a>. Same <code>*</code>, same limit, and nobody mentioned <code>sshd</code>.</p>
<h2 id="the-bug-in-six-lines">The bug in six lines</h2>
<p>Without PAM, <code>sshd</code> checked passwords like this. I shortened the names from <a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/commit/4353fdc43ef646c43a8dda7025cc1a898caf1482" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>auth-passwd.c</code></a>:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">char</span><span class="hl-k"> *</span><span class="hl-fg">stored </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-f"> shadow_pw</span><span class="hl-fg">(pw);</span><span class="hl-cm">          /* "*" */</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">char</span><span class="hl-k"> *</span><span class="hl-fg">salt </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-c"> NULL</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">if</span><span class="hl-fg"> (valid </span><span class="hl-k">&#x26;&#x26;</span><span class="hl-fg"> stored[</span><span class="hl-c">0</span><span class="hl-fg">] </span><span class="hl-k">&#x26;&#x26;</span><span class="hl-fg"> stored[</span><span class="hl-c">1</span><span class="hl-fg">])</span><span class="hl-cm">   /* one character: not a salt */</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-fg">        salt </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-fg"> stored;</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">char</span><span class="hl-k"> *</span><span class="hl-fg">out </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-f"> xcrypt</span><span class="hl-fg">(password</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-fg"> salt);</span><span class="hl-cm">    /* no salt: borrow one */</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">return</span><span class="hl-fg"> out </span><span class="hl-k">!=</span><span class="hl-c"> NULL</span><span class="hl-k"> &#x26;&#x26;</span><span class="hl-f"> strcmp</span><span class="hl-fg">(out</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-fg"> stored) </span><span class="hl-k">==</span><span class="hl-c"> 0</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p>Step a 300-byte password through it:</p>
<div data-article-component="Diagram" data-state="fallback"><figure class="not-prose pb-3 article-frame my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] text-syntax-text"><div class="flex min-h-11 items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b px-3 font-mono text-xs text-syntax-text border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><span aria-hidden="false">diagram · 6 steps</span></div><div class="px-3 py-6 outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-inset"><svg role="img" aria-labelledby="_R_2bep6ba_-title _R_2bep6ba_-desc" viewBox="0 0 560 380" class="mx-auto block h-auto w-full max-w-xl"><title id="_R_2bep6ba_-title">One 300-byte password through sshd without PAM, on glibc and on musl</title><desc id="_R_2bep6ba_-desc">Three shared steps, then two lanes. A 300-byte password arrives for an account whose shadow field is *. The field is too short to be a salt, so sshd borrows a $6$ salt from another account. On glibc, crypt() returns a real hash, which does not equal *, and the login is rejected. On musl, crypt() refuses keys over 256 bytes and returns *, which equals the stored field, and the login is accepted.</desc><g data-part="arrive" data-state="all" class="group/part transition-opacity duration-300 ease-out data-[state=off]:opacity-30 motion-reduce:transition-none"><rect x="12" y="12" width="168" height="52" class="fill-card stroke-border transition-[stroke] duration-300 group-data-[state=on]/part:stroke-primary motion-reduce:transition-none" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="24" y="33" font-size="13" class="fill-foreground font-mono">1. password</text><text x="24" y="51" font-size="11" class="fill-muted-foreground font-mono">300 bytes for deploy</text></g><line x1="180" y1="38" x2="188" y2="38" class="fill-none stroke-muted-foreground transition-[stroke] duration-300 group-data-[state=on]/part:stroke-primary motion-reduce:transition-none" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="196,38 187,33.5 187,42.5" class="fill-muted-foreground transition-[fill] durati