 y="175" font-size="11" class="fill-muted-foreground font-mono">over 256 bytes: fails, returns *</text></g><line x1="420" y1="188" x2="420" y2="204" class="fill-none stroke-muted-foreground transition-[stroke] duration-300 group-data-[state=on]/part:stroke-primary motion-reduce:transition-none" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="420,212 415.5,203 424.5,203" class="fill-muted-foreground transition-[fill] duration-300 group-data-[state=on]/part:fill-primary motion-reduce:transition-none"/><g data-part="musl-cmp" data-state="all" class="group/part transition-opacity duration-300 ease-out data-[state=off]:opacity-30 motion-reduce:transition-none"><rect x="292" y="212" width="256" height="52" class="fill-card stroke-border transition-[stroke] duration-300 group-data-[state=on]/part:stroke-primary motion-reduce:transition-none" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="304" y="233" font-size="13" class="fill-foreground font-mono">5. strcmp(result, stored)</text><text x="304" y="251" font-size="11" class="fill-muted-foreground font-mono">* = *</text></g><line x1="420" y1="264" x2="420" y2="280" class="fill-none stroke-muted-foreground transition-[stroke] duration-300 group-data-[state=on]/part:stroke-primary motion-reduce:transition-none" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="420,288 415.5,279 424.5,279" class="fill-muted-foreground transition-[fill] duration-300 group-data-[state=on]/part:fill-primary motion-reduce:transition-none"/><g data-part="musl-result" data-state="all" class="group/part transition-opacity duration-300 ease-out data-[state=off]:opacity-30 motion-reduce:transition-none"><rect x="292" y="288" width="256" height="44" class="fill-card stroke-destructive" stroke-width="2"/><text x="304" y="314.5" font-size="13" class="fill-destructive font-mono">Accepted password</text></g><text x="12" y="368" font-size="11" class="fill-muted-foreground font-mono">Only step 4 differs. On musl the error string and the stored field are the same.</text></svg></div><ol class="grid gap-px border-t px-1 pt-2 border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><li><div class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-baseline gap-2 px-2 py-1.5 text-left text-sm leading-relaxed border-l-2 border-transparent"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">01</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">A password arrives<!-- -->.</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">Someone sends 300 bytes as the password for deploy, an account that has * in /etc/shadow. sshd accepts up to 1,024 bytes.</span></span></div></li><li><div class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-baseline gap-2 px-2 py-1.5 text-left text-sm leading-relaxed border-l-2 border-transparent"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">02</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">The stored field is not a salt<!-- -->.</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">sshd uses the stored field as the salt when it has at least two characters. * has one, so sshd has no salt of its own.</span></span></div></li><li><div class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-baseline gap-2 px-2 py-1.5 text-left text-sm leading-relaxed border-l-2 border-transparent"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">03</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">sshd borrows a salt<!-- -->.</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">Since 2016, sshd hashes with a salt taken from another account, so a failed login takes as long as a real one. Here it finds bob&#x27;s $6$ salt.</span></span></div></li><li><div class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-baseline gap-2 px-2 py-1.5 text-left text-sm leading-relaxed border-l-2 border-transparent"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">04</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">crypt() runs<!-- -->.</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">glibc hashes the 300 bytes like any other password. musl&#x27;s sha512 crypt refuses keys over 256 bytes and returns its error string, *.</span></span></div></li><li><div class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-baseline gap-2 px-2 py-1.5 text-left text-sm leading-relaxed border-l-2 border-transparent"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">05</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">strcmp() compares<!-- -->.</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">sshd compares what crypt() returned with the stored field. On glibc that is a full $6$ hash against *. On musl it is * against *.</span></span></div></li><li><div class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-baseline gap-2 px-2 py-1.5 text-left text-sm leading-relaxed border-l-2 border-transparent"><span class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">06</span><span><span class="text-syntax-text">The verdict<!-- -->.</span> <span class="text-syntax-comment">glibc rejects the login. musl accepts it, for any password from 257 to 1,024 bytes.</span></span></div></li></ol><figcaption class="mt-3 px-3 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-syntax-comment">one 300-byte password, glibc and musl</figcaption></figure><noscript><p class="whitespace-pre-line">One 300-byte password through sshd without PAM, on glibc and on musl. Three shared steps, then two lanes. A 300-byte password arrives for an account whose shadow field is *. The field is too short to be a salt, so sshd borrows a $6$ salt from another account. On glibc, crypt() returns a real hash, which does not equal *, and the login is rejected. On musl, crypt() refuses keys over 256 bytes and returns *, which equals the stored field, and the login is accepted.</p></noscript></div>
<p>I didn&#x27;t want to take the libc part on trust, so I ran <code>crypt()</code> on real musl and on real libxcrypt, in <code>alpine:3.24</code> and <code>ubuntu:24.04</code> containers:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">#define</span><span class="hl-f"> _GNU_SOURCE</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">#include</span><span class="hl-se"> &#x3C;stdio.h></span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">#include</span><span class="hl-se"> &#x3C;stdlib.h></span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">#include</span><span class="hl-se"> &#x3C;string.h></span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">#include</span><span class="hl-se"> &#x3C;unistd.h></span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">int</span><span class="hl-f"> main</span><span class="hl-fg">(</span><span class="hl-k">void</span><span class="hl-fg">) {</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">    char</span><span class="hl-fg"> key[</span><span class="hl-c">1025</span><span class="hl-fg">];</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">    memset(key</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-se"> 'A'</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-c"> 256</span><span class="hl-f">)</span><span class="hl-fg">; key[</span><span class="hl-c">256</span><span class="hl-fg">] </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-c"> 0</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">    puts(crypt(key</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-se"> "$6$saltsalt$"</span><span class="hl-f">))</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span><span class="hl-cm">   /* 256 bytes: a hash */</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">    memset(key</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-se"> 'A'</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-c"> 257</span><span class="hl-f">)</span><span class="hl-fg">; key[</span><span class="hl-c">257</span><span class="hl-fg">] </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-c"> 0</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">    puts(crypt(key</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-se"> "$6$saltsalt$"</span><span class="hl-f">))</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span><span class="hl-cm">   /* 257 bytes: "*" on musl */</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">    puts(crypt(key</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-se"> "*"</span><span class="hl-f">))</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span><span class="hl-cm">              /* a bare star as setting: "x" */</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-fg">}</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><div class="mb-3 flex items-baseline gap-4 border-b border-dashed pr-3 pb-3 pl-3 font-mono text-xs"><span class="min-w-0 truncate text-syntax-text">terminal</span><span class="text-syntax-comment max-sm:hidden">2<!-- --> <!-- -->lines</span><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary ml-auto">copy</button></div><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">docker</span><span class="hl-s"> run</span><span class="hl-s"> --rm</span><span class="hl-s"> -v</span><span class="hl-se"> "$PWD"</span><span class="hl-s">:/w</span><span class="hl-s"> -w</span><span class="hl-s"> /w</span><span class="hl-s"> alpine:3.24</span><span class="hl-fg"> \</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-s">  sh</span><span class="hl-s"> -c</span><span class="hl-se"> 'apk add -q build-base &#x26;&#x26; cc star.c -o star &#x26;&#x26; ./star'</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p>One extra byte turns a full hash into a single star. The wider run, 7 to 1,024 bytes:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Password</th><th>musl</th><th>glibc with libxcrypt</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$6$saltsalt$</code></td><td>256 bytes</td><td>real hash</td><td>real hash</td></tr><tr><td><code>$6$saltsalt$</code></td><td>257 bytes</td><td><code>*</code></td><td>real hash</td></tr><tr><td><code>$5$saltsalt$</code></td><td>257 bytes</td><td><code>*</code></td><td>real hash</td></tr><tr><td><code>$6$saltsalt$</code></td><td>512 bytes</td><td><code>*</code></td><td><code>*0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>$1$saltsalt$</code></td><td>1,024 bytes</td><td>real hash</td><td><code>*0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>xx</code> (DES)</td><td>1,024 bytes</td><td>real hash</td><td><code>*0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>any length</td><td><code>x</code></td><td><code>*0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>*LK*</code></td><td>any length</td><td>DES hash starting <code>*L</code></td><td><code>*0</code></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The table explains the odd numbers in the advisories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Why 257 bytes:</strong> the first length past musl&#x27;s limit.</li>
<li><strong>Why 1,024 bytes:</strong> <code>MAX_PASSWORD_LEN</code> in <code>sshd</code>, which rejects anything longer before it hashes.</li>
<li><strong>Why glibc is safe:</strong> libxcrypt fails from 512 bytes too, but its error string is <code>*0</code>, and <code>*0</code> isn&#x27;t <code>*</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Why <code>!</code> is safe:</strong> <code>crypt()</code> still returns <code>*</code>, and <code>*</code> isn&#x27;t <code>!</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Why <code>*LK*</code> is safe:</strong> it has four characters, so <code>sshd</code> uses it as the salt, and musl computes an ordinary DES hash that starts <code>*L</code> and can&#x27;t equal <code>*LK*</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Try it yourself. This runs the same decisions in your browser with stand-in hashes, and nothing leaves the page:</p>
<div data-article-component="StarLogin" data-state="fallback"><section aria-label="Try it" class="not-prose article-frame my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] text-syntax-text"><div class="not-prose flex min-h-11 items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b px-3 font-mono text-xs border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><span class="min-w-0 truncate text-syntax-text">try it · log in as deploy</span><span class="flex shrink-0 items-center text-syntax-comment tabular-nums">5 cases</span></div><table class="w-full font-mono text-xs"><thead><tr class="text-left text-syntax-comment"><th class="py-2 pr-4 pl-3 font-normal">password</th><th class="py-2 pr-4 font-normal">Debian · glibc + libxcrypt</th><th class="py-2 pr-4 font-normal">Alpine · musl</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-t border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><td class="py-2 pr-4 pl-3 tabular-nums">7<!-- --> bytes</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">a hash → denied</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">a hash → denied</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><td class="py-2 pr-4 pl-3 tabular-nums">256<!-- --> bytes</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">a hash → denied</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">a hash → denied</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><td class="py-2 pr-4 pl-3 tabular-nums">257<!-- --> bytes</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">a hash → denied</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-destructive">&quot;*&quot; → accepted</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><td class="py-2 pr-4 pl-3 tabular-nums">1024<!-- --> bytes</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">&quot;*0&quot; → denied</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-destructive">&quot;*&quot; → accepted</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-dashed border-syntax-gutter/40"><td class="py-2 pr-4 pl-3 tabular-nums">1025<!-- --> bytes</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">never hashed → denied</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">never hashed → denied</td></tr></tbody></table></section></div>
<p>I tested the libc here, not a full login against a live <code>sshd</code>. The login path comes from reading the code, the write-up and Alpine&#x27;s advisory.</p>
<h2 id="the-fix-and-whose-bug-it-is">The fix, and whose bug it is</h2>
<p>The security part of <a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/commit/4353fdc43ef646c43a8dda7025cc1a898caf1482" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the OpenSSH commit</a> is one new condition:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-k">#define</span><span class="hl-f"> MIN_PASSWORD_HASH_LEN</span><span class="hl-c"> 4</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-fg">ret </span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-fg"> encrypted_password </span><span class="hl-k">!=</span><span class="hl-c"> NULL</span><span class="hl-k"> &#x26;&#x26;</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">    strlen</span><span class="hl-fg">(pw_password) </span><span class="hl-k">>=</span><span class="hl-fg"> MIN_PASSWORD_HASH_LEN </span><span class="hl-k">&#x26;&#x26;</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">    strcmp</span><span class="hl-fg">(encrypted_password</span><span class="hl-p">,</span><span class="hl-fg"> pw_password) </span><span class="hl-k">==</span><span class="hl-c"> 0</span><span class="hl-fg">;</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p>A stored field shorter than four characters now can&#x27;t match anything. <code>sshd</code> still runs <code>crypt()</code> first, so the 2016 timing fix stays in place.</p>
<p>Neither side claims the root cause. The commit message says: &quot;We do not consider the root cause to be within OpenSSH&quot;. Felker&#x27;s <a href="https://www.openwall.com/lists/musl/2026/11/05/4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">advisory to musl users</a> says: &quot;It is my position that this is not a bug in musl just an erroneous use by the caller.&quot;</p>
<p>Both have a case:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Against musl:</strong> the specification says <code>crypt()</code> returns NULL on failure. <code>sshd</code> checked for NULL, so a change made to protect careless programs took the signal away from a careful one.</li>
<li><strong>Against OpenSSH:</strong> musl&#x27;s guard promises that the result never equals the setting. Since 2016, <code>sshd</code> has compared the result with a different string, and that promise never covered it.</li>
</ul>
<p>I think each side kept its own contract and the failure sits between them. The last change to musl&#x27;s crypt code in a <a href="https://github.com/kraj/musl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mirror of its history</a> dates from 2020, and the mirror runs to 25 October, so <code>*</code> was still the error value then. <code>sshd</code> is the only known victim, but any program on musl that hashes with one string and compares with another has the same problem.</p>
<h2 id="one-day-no-embargo-and-a-lot-of-agents">One day, no embargo, and a lot of agents</h2>
<p>OpenSSH learned of the bug the day before it shipped the fix. The commit is dated 5 November at 08:18 UTC+11, the evening of 4 November in UTC. Damien Miller <a href="https://lists.mindrot.org/pipermail/openssh-unix-dev/2026-November/042837.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">explained the speed</a>:</p>
<blockquote class="blockquote">
<p>We are making this release as fast as possible (we learned of the problem yesterday) because we have recently observed multiple cases of rapid bug rediscovery by AI agents, sometimes within hours, and so now consider long embargo periods to be a dangerous practice that primarily benefits attackers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>OpenSSH had said as much a month earlier. The <a href="https://seclists.org/oss-sec/2026/q4/58" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">10.6 announcement</a> of 6 October noted that bugs found by AI tools were being found again by other researchers, which suggests attackers who never report bugs can find them too.</p>
<p>The discovery method is the part I keep thinking about. Kültekin built <code>sshd</code> every way possible: glibc and musl, with and without PAM, with and without shadow. Then came about 17,600 odd logins against the builds. The result fits in one line of the write-up: &quot;every build agreed on every login. except musl without pam.&quot; On who did the work: &quot;i had agent swarms. a lot of them.&quot;</p>
<p>Differential testing is an old idea, and agents made it cheap. Kültekin didn&#x27;t have to suspect <code>crypt()</code>. The builds disagreed, and the disagreement pointed at the bug.</p>
<p>Code review alone missed it. On 30 October, <a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/commit/84f8f4666b536dcd3959ab27171caecd5e80947b" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a commit</a> fixed <code>pick_salt()</code> for bcrypt hashes, crediting the report Anthropic ANT-2026-7T6M8N4X. That change touched the salt logic at the centre of this story, and the bypass stayed. No single file looks wrong. The bug shows up only when all the parts run together.</p>
<p>Fast disclosure has a cost, and small operators pay it. The full write-up went public on the day of the fix, so admins had hours. I found no reports of exploitation and no count of exposed hosts so far, which doesn&#x27;t say much on day one.</p>
<h2 id="what-to-do-now">What to do now</h2>
<p><strong>1. Update and restart.</strong> On Alpine:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><div class="mb-3 flex items-baseline gap-4 border-b border-dashed pr-3 pb-3 pl-3 font-mono text-xs"><span class="min-w-0 truncate text-syntax-text">terminal</span><span class="text-syntax-comment max-sm:hidden">2<!-- --> <!-- -->lines</span><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary ml-auto">copy</button></div><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">apk</span><span class="hl-s"> upgrade</span><span class="hl-s"> -U</span><span class="hl-s"> -a</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">rc-service</span><span class="hl-s"> sshd</span><span class="hl-s"> restart</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p>Don&#x27;t go by the upstream version number. Alpine backported the patch, so a fixed 3.24 box still says 10.3. <a href="https://alpinelinux.org/posts/2026-11-05-openssh-password-authentication-bypass/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Alpine&#x27;s advisory</a> lists these fixed packages:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Alpine branch</th><th>Fixed openssh package</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>edge</td><td>10.6_p2-r0</td></tr><tr><td>3.24</td><td>10.3_p1-r2</td></tr><tr><td>3.23</td><td>10.2_p1-r1</td></tr><tr><td>3.22</td><td>10.0_p1-r11</td></tr><tr><td>3.21</td><td>9.9_p2-r1</td></tr><tr><td>3.20</td><td>9.7_p1-r6</td></tr><tr><td>3.19</td><td>9.6_p1-r3</td></tr><tr><td>3.18</td><td>9.3_p2-r4</td></tr><tr><td>3.17</td><td>9.1_p1-r7</td></tr><tr><td>3.16</td><td>9.0_p1-r6</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Alpine 3.21 and older are past their end of support, 3.21 since 1 November. The fixed packages are there, but plan to move off those branches.</p>
<p>On other musl systems, build OpenSSH 10.6p2 or wait for your vendor. OpenWrt&#x27;s OpenSSH package <a href="https://github.com/openwrt/packages/pull/31158" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">moved to 10.6p2</a> the same day. Turn password logins off until then, and rebuild any images you ship that contain <code>sshd</code>.</p>
<p><strong>2. If you can&#x27;t update, turn password logins off.</strong> First check that your key works, and keep your current session open:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><div class="mb-3 flex items-baseline gap-4 border-b border-dashed pr-3 pb-3 pl-3 font-mono text-xs"><span class="min-w-0 truncate text-syntax-text">terminal</span><span class="text-syntax-comment max-sm:hidden">4<!-- --> <!-- -->lines</span><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary ml-auto">copy</button></div><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">mkdir</span><span class="hl-s"> -p</span><span class="hl-s"> /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">echo</span><span class="hl-se"> 'PasswordAuthentication no'</span><span class="hl-k"> ></span><span class="hl-s"> /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-no-passwords.conf</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">sshd</span><span class="hl-s"> -t</span><span class="hl-p"> &#x26;&#x26;</span><span class="hl-f"> rc-service</span><span class="hl-s"> sshd</span><span class="hl-s"> restart</span></span></span></span><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">sshd</span><span class="hl-s"> -T</span><span class="hl-k"> |</span><span class="hl-f"> grep</span><span class="hl-s"> -i</span><span class="hl-s"> passwordauthentication</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p>The last command must print <code>passwordauthentication no</code>. Alpine&#x27;s default config reads that directory only from 3.19 onward. On older releases, or with <code>Match</code> blocks, use the <code>sed</code> command in the advisory.</p>
<p><strong>3. Check exposed machines for a break-in.</strong> Look for password logins to accounts that have no password:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><div class="mb-3 flex items-baseline gap-4 border-b border-dashed pr-3 pb-3 pl-3 font-mono text-xs"><span class="min-w-0 truncate text-syntax-text">terminal</span><span class="text-syntax-comment max-sm:hidden">1<!-- --> <!-- -->line</span><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary ml-auto">copy</button></div><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-f">grep</span><span class="hl-se"> 'sshd.*Accepted password for'</span><span class="hl-s"> /var/log/messages</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p>Such a line for <code>alpine</code>, <code>root</code> or a key-only user is a bad sign. Also review <code>doas</code> use, new users, new files and <code>authorized_keys</code>. Your logs may not go back far, and the bug is ten years old. Alpine&#x27;s advice: if you can&#x27;t rule out a login, treat the machine as compromised.</p>
<p><strong>4. Don&#x27;t rule yourself out on a technicality.</strong> The conditions above are the ones published today. If your server runs <code>sshd</code> on musl without PAM, update it even if one condition looks false.</p>
<h2 id="what-i-take-from-it">What I take from it</h2>
<p><code>*</code> meant &quot;no login&quot; in the data and &quot;failure&quot; in a return value. Those two meanings lived apart from 2012 to 2016, until one comparison put them on either side of a <code>strcmp</code>. Any <code>-1</code>, <code>&quot;&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;unknown&quot;</code> your code returns as an error can meet a stored value the same way.</p>
<p>musl promised that the result never equals the setting. <code>sshd</code> needed a different promise: that the result never equals the stored field. The two were the same until a timing fix separated them. When you change what you pass into a function, check what its guarantees still cover.</p>
<p>A <code>*</code> in <code>/etc/shadow</code> feels like &quot;this account has no password&quot;, but <code>sshd</code> only ever saw a string to compare. If you want no password logins, set <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code> and let the server refuse them before any hash runs.</p>
